ther  languages went through similar processes  and are therefore much less cluttered  than a normal language is now these  languages can express any nuance of  human thought but they haven't existed  long enough to also dangle unnecessary  things like willfully irregular verbs  these are called creole languages it's  far easier to manage a basic  conversation in a creole than in any  older language  haitian creole for example is a language  low on the complications that make  learning navajo or hmong so tough  it spares a student from having to know  that both or male and tablets are female  which is one of the reasons that it's so  hard to master french the language from  which it got most of its word creola  languages were created worldwide during  the era that the textbooks call  western exploration african soldiers  created an arabic crayola in sudan  orphans created a german one in new  guinea aboriginal australians created an  english creole which was passed on to  surrounding locations such as again new  guinea where under the name takbisin  it is today the language of government  for people speaking hundreds of  different native languages jamaican  patois  south carolina's gula and cape verdean  are other examples modern population  movements are now creating a third wave  of language streamlining  in cities worldwide children of  immigrants speaking many different  languages or growing up speaking among  themselves or version of their new  country's language it's a kind of  compromise between the original version  of the language and the way their  parents speak it linguists have no  single term yet for these new speech  varieties but from kies deutsch in  germany to kebab norsk in norway from  the urban wall of of senegal to  singapore singlish the world is  witnessing the birth of lightly  optimized versions of old languages this  streamlining should not be taken as a  sign of decline  all of the optimized languages remain  full languages in every sense 